WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:05.310
The Fast and Furious franchise is famous for
its high-octane car chases and spectacular

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:09.280
crashes, but are all of those wild stunts
really as dangerous as they look?

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:09.780
Absolutely.

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:14.220
Stunt driver and coordinator Robert Nagle,
who has worked on every installment in the

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:18.990
Fast and Furious series since Fast Five, and
recently oversaw the thrilling racing scenes

00:00:18.990 --> 00:00:24.250
in James Mangold's Oscar-nominated film Ford
v Ferrari, spoke with Looper in an exclusive

00:00:24.250 --> 00:00:25.660
interview about his career.

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:29.579
Before he became a stunt driver, he worked
as a racer — though he switched careers

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:31.400
when money became too big of a factor.

00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:32.700
Nagle explained,

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:37.280
"Going down the back straightaway at 180 miles
an hour and you're trying to think about where

00:00:37.289 --> 00:00:41.000
the next check is coming from... it just takes
away from the passion of the sport."

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:45.000
This is why Nagle tends not to worry about
whether or not he and his fellow drivers are

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:45.880
up to the task.,

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:50.260
"A lot of the guys I hire have a pretty solid
race background, because I know I can put

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:51.100
them in any car.

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:54.300
Still, sometimes even the best drivers can
lose control.

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:59.100
Ford v Ferrari's most spectacular car crash,
which occurs as Christian Bale's Ken Miles

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:03.800
completes his very first lap at the 24 Hours
of Le Mans, was based on a real-life event.

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:08.540
Before working on Ford v Ferrari, the film's
second unit director, Darrin Prescott, was

00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:12.840
participating in a celebrity race when another
car fell apart right in front of him.

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:16.660
"It was a right-hand corner and the car went
a little bit wide and dropped the outside

00:01:16.670 --> 00:01:21.140
wheels into the grass, and that turned the
car sideways and shot it back across the track

00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:22.520
and it hit a concrete wall.

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:25.060
[Prescott] was pretty certain he just saw
somebody die."

00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:28.000
Luckily, Prescott was wrong, and the other
driver was fine.

00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:32.880
When it came time to film Ford v Ferrari,
Nagle and Prescott decided to recreate that

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:33.500
crash in the movie.

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:37.360
However, the final scene isn't there just
for thrills, but to show how dangerous Le

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:37.860
Mans is.

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:41.640
After all, the wreck comes during the first
four minutes of Miles' race, when he still

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:43.180
has 24 hours to go.

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:47.920
On set though, the real danger often comes
from factors outside of the stunt team's control.

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:51.850
When asked to identify his most dangerous
stunt on the Fast and Furious films, Nagle

00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:54.780
explained that almost everything is inherently
unsafe, saying,

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:56.660
"There's so much going on with some of these…

00:01:56.670 --> 00:02:00.780
[There are] multiple cars... and explosions,
pyrotechnics going off around us.

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:04.010
There's a whole lot going on and a whole lot
that can go wrong very quickly."

00:02:04.010 --> 00:02:08.570
Still, when pressed, Nagle singled out one
Fast and Furious scene that was especially

00:02:08.570 --> 00:02:12.880
dangerous: the opening of Fast and Furious
6, which he described as "pretty hairball."

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:17.200
In the sequence, Dominic Toretto, played by
Vin Diesel, and Brian O'Conner, played by

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:21.780
Paul Walker, race on a twisty Spanish road
in order to get home before Brian's girlfriend,

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:24.620
Mia, played by Jordana Brewster, gives birth.

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:28.500
On one side is a sheer rock cliff; on the
other, nothing but ocean.

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:32.340
The scene was filmed on-location on Tenerife,
the largest of the Canary Islands.

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:37.020
Nagle was in the camera car filming the action,
while former Top Gear driver Ben Collins and

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:40.060
rally car champion Mark Higgins did the actual
racing.

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:43.540
As Nagle explained, the driving wasn't the
problem — the road was.

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:47.440
"There was this road that they wanted me to
go shoot that was deemed too dangerous for

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:49.100
the general public to drive on.

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:52.880
It's got a thousand-foot drop off into the
ocean with these little blocks that will probably

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:56.780
slow you down just enough so you get a really
good view of where you're about to crash."

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:01.760
"This time it ain't just about being fast."

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:07.780
In addition to the terrain of the road itself,
there was another hazard that no amount of

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:10.020
careful planning or skill could account for.

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:11.180
According to Nagle,

00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:14.520
"I commented to the guys, 'Some really bad
potholes on that road.'

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:18.740
Somebody [said] to me, 'You know, those aren't
potholes those are divots from rocks.

00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:22.180
They've been falling off the cliff on the
other side of you that's a thousand feet up.'"

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:26.040
As if the road conditions weren't already
dangerous enough, Nagle also had to navigate

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:28.040
it at full speed without any practice.

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:31.660
While Collins and Higgins had been racing
for a week by the time they filmed the stunt,

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:33.680
it was Nagle's first day at work.

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:34.640
Nagle admitted,

00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:39.100
"Two very top drivers that had been driving
this road for the last week and I've got to...

00:03:39.100 --> 00:03:40.760
chase them down with this camera car.

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:42.160
Yeah, it was a little scary."

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:46.700
Fortunately, Nagle's skill and experience
paid off, and he was able to film the scene

00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:47.440
in one take.

